# C++ Competitive Programming Style

Apply this to **new and edited contest solutions** (`.cc` / `.cpp`). Do not mass-reformat unrelated files. Library headers (`lib/*.hh`) follow the Library section instead.

Target **C++17** (Codeforces GNU G++17 / C++20). Prefer standard, defined behavior. No compiler extensions except repo `bits/stdc++.h` in standalone solutions.

Format with the repo `.clang-format`: LLVM, 2-space indent, 100 columns, braces attach, `int* x` (pointer left).

## File layout

Name contest files `{letter}_{snake_case_title}.cc`, e.g. `f_building_tree.cc`. Put them under the contest folder (`codeforces/spectral/R1/`, …).

Standalone solutions start with a **sketch**, then includes, then an anonymous namespace, then `main`:

```cpp
// Platform Contest — Problem Title
// https://judge-url/problem/X
//
// Sketch
// ------
// What is being computed, the key formula, the algorithm, and why it is fast
// enough. Include the complexity.
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

namespace {
// types and helpers
}  // namespace

int main() {
  ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
  cin.tie(nullptr);
  // read, solve, write
  return 0;
}
```

Use repo `bits/stdc++.h` when the system header is missing. Do **not** paste long STL include lists into solutions.

**Contest submissions must be self-contained.** Inline DSU/FFT/… in the solution file. Do not `#include "../../../lib/..."`.

## Comments

- File-header sketch is required for non-trivial problems (the “why”, not a line-by-line restatement).
- Comment non-obvious invariants, formulas, and UB-sensitive code.
- Do not narrate `i++`.

## Naming and types

- Types: `PascalCase`. Functions, variables, files: `snake_case`.
- Constants: `kPascalCase` or `constexpr` (never `#define maxn`).
- Private members: trailing underscore (`cost_`).
- 64-bit integers: **`int64_t`**, not `long long`, `ll`, or `i64`.
- `nullptr`, not `NULL` or `0`. Prefer `const` on non-mutating locals, parameters, and methods.

## Functions and constructors

Put **all parameters on one line** when they fit in the 100-column limit. If they do not fit, wrap **one parameter per line** — never bin-pack a few on the first line and the rest on the next.

```cpp
// GOOD — fits
bool unite(int a, int b)

// BAD — wrapped but bin-packed
Solver(int vertex_count, int m, const vector<pair<int, int>>& endpoints,
       const vector<int>& weights, const vector<int>& query_colors)

// GOOD — does not fit, so one per line
Solver(
    int vertex_count,
    int m,
    const vector<pair<int, int>>& endpoints,
    const vector<int>& weights,
    const vector<int>& query_colors)
```

Same rule for call sites and constructor initializer lists: one line if it fits, otherwise one item per line.

Mark single-argument constructors `explicit`.

Do not evaluate a value and `std::move` the same object in one argument list — argument order is unspecified (MSVC often right-to-left). Pass sizes/counts as separate parameters.

Use `std::move`, not unqualified `move`.

## Structure (SWE)

- Put helpers in `namespace { ... }`.
- Prefer small structs with named methods over global arrays and `#define`.
- Keep data members private when the type has invariants; expose `find` / `unite` / `size`, not raw `parent[]`.
- Prefer STL (`vector`, `iota`) over C arrays for new solutions.
- Reset **all** per-test state in multi-test problems (adjacency lists, timestamps, static arrays).
- Convert to 0-based at input; convert back only if the judge wants 1-based output.
- Fast I/O as above. Use `'\n'` in batch problems; `endl` only when the protocol requires a flush (interactive).

## Correctness

- Stay inside constraints; state complexity in the sketch and meet TL/ML.
- Avoid UB: OOB, uninit reads, signed overflow, dangling references, invalid iterator use.
- Watch integer overflow: use `int64_t` for products and answers that can exceed `2e9`.
- Interactive: flush every query. Multiple tests: do not reuse `visited` without a timestamp/clear.

## Verify

Run the official sample (and a brute on small random cases when the idea is delicate) before calling the task done.

## Library (`lib/*.hh`) and tests

- Explicit includes, `std::` qualification, named namespace (e.g. `delaunay2d`). No `bits/stdc++.h`, no `using namespace std`.
- Tests: Google Test in `tests/`, `cxx_test` in `tests/BUCK`, helpers in `tests/support/`.

## Exceptions

- IOI/grader headers (`race.h`, …) stay **before** `bits/stdc++.h`.
- TopCoder class shells: do not rewrite the published API.
- Do not mass-rewrite old `maxn` / `i64` files unless the user asked.
